00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
hi  i'm simona and i work at hiku and this  presentation is about  learning from your model's mistakes  first few words on data cure the taiko  has been founded in 2013  by four french guys and it's today  headquartered in the us  the reiko provides a data science  platform uh which works  across all industries across the globe  it's for enterprise ai  and since last year that iko is also a  unicorn  we're growing we're currently hiring so  don't hesitate to have a look at the  offers on our website  and so machine learning models make  mistakes  and model debugging and error analysis  are necessary steps in the iterative  process of model design  any data scientist after training a  machine learning model needs to  investigate  its failures to understand which  subpopulation the model is performing  poorly  he needs to build intuition about what  are the problematic features correlated  with the error and what are the  feature feature values typical of  failures  usually this kind of analysis is  performed manually but this is  ineffective and time consuming  so here we focus on how to provide data  scientists with an automatic  tool to simplify model error analysis  the first key step behind model error  analysis is to break down the error  because there might be several reasons  why a machine learning model is  making mistakes and if we try to  treat all the error as a whole it's  actually quite difficult to make sense  of it  if you um imagine to take all the  misclassified force in the popular mnist  dataset for instance  it's very difficult to understand what's  going on there but if you consider  if you group the different predictions  by predicted class you could  make sense of the error that is behind  it for instance some force they look  like nine some four look like seven etc  so actually segmenting the errors in  meaningful clusters  allows to have an easier analysis  and actually model performance  predictors are also mpp  they can help automatize this step  so let's see how performance predictor  work  first we have a primary model under  investigation so it's the primary model  we're studying for instance you can take  a  primary model that is predicting the  price of houses  and so this primary model also has an  associated set  and we compute the prediction of the  primary model on the test set and we  categorize it  as either a success or a failure  then we have a secondary model that is  our model performance predictor  so it's a binary classification model  that is trained on the test set features  and also on this binary categorization  of the prediction as either a success or  a failure  and so this secondary model is trained  to predict whether the model the primary  model succeeds  or whether it fails usually we take a  decision tree classifier as a model  performance predictor  because it's easier to interpret and so  this decision  tree would uh will cluster different  type of errors in  failure segments those are the the  decision tree nodes  and so each node group samples with  similar features some other performances  and actually a fellow not represents a  specific  different kind of error and so those are  meant to be studied individually  and to simplify the analysis  model performance predictor exists for  both classification and regression tasks  if we have a classification  classification task  the failure is defined as a wrong  predictor class  but we could also go deeper in the  analysis and maybe categorize  any failed predictions as  like a false positive or a false  negative per class  if we have a regression task the failure  is defined a bit different is a large  deviation of the prediction from the  actual value  and this definition requires also  defining a tolerance threshold  above which the deviation defines a  failure  and this is computed as a knee point of  the regression error characteristic  curve or the regression model  and this allows actually that the  absolute error of primary prediction  stays within tolerable limits for those  predictions that we defined as success  also here in regression we could  consider more levels of error and not  only a  binary threshold  now we have built a model performance  predictor and we need also to gather  information from it together some  insights about  the error behind  so the mvp can actually have a complex  structures  many nodes and we need to highlight the  data that is relevant to failure and  discard all the rest  so how can we do this automatically  first we can select the most important  failure nodes by  size and purity and then when we have a  look at one specific failure node  we can look at the features correlated  with the error  so those ones we can gather them  for instance by ranking the features by  the performance predictor feature  importance  which in case of a decision tree is the  mean increase of impurity  but we can also have a finer analysis if  we take  importance measure that are local to the  node  and so those gives us those give us  the critical features for a specific  group of errors  and one example of doing this is to use  the mean shot values of the samples  inside that fall under a specific  failure node  one important tool to get insights from  the  model performance predictor is this  discriminating discriminative local  versus global  analysis in particular we can compare  all the symbols that are in a failure  node  against the global population which is  mainly successful  if we've done a good job with the model  and so this could be like a baseline a  background  against which we compare what's going on  in the fader node  so if we look at the distribution of the  critical features in the node versus  this global successful population  if there are differences we can have an  idea  what are the subpopulation on which the  model is failing like in this example if  we take back the example of a model  a primary model predicting the price of  houses here we could  understand that the model is failing to  predict the price  for houses with very large living rooms  maybe because  these are underrepresented in our  training set  we are using models that usually they  are trained on preprocessed features and  actually pre-process features are not  good for interpretability as you can  guess  and in general model debugging on  preprocessed data is not ideal  so what we need to do is to use um some  tools for instance circular pipeline  to undo this preprocessing and to  visualize  the human readable feature values and  this allows to have a  clear explanation what's going on for  instance in this same illustration that  we have here as before about  the surface of the living rooms we have  negative numbers so it could be  even a little bit misleading and we can  be puzzled by this  visualization instead if we can undo the  preprocessing we will have a  clear human readable value  and so now it's time to connect the dots  and put all together  if you are interested in modulator  analysis you can  perform this procedure i explained in  this presentation but you can also  use an open source package that we are  publishing soon  that uh for provides an error analysis  tool for secular models  and it also supports pipeline models so  it  provides an error analyzer to analyze  the model and train a  model performance predictor and also  another visualizer to display  the performance predictor tree and also  feature distributions  as i said it supports pipeline so you  can in fu line of codes you can  instantiate an error analyzer on both  encyclone or a pipeline model and  to train um to fit it assets to train  and model performance predictor  and afterward you can use the visualizer  to visualize  both the error tree and features  distributions  and this package automatically the  failure nodes  more important they are highlighted and  also the feature that are more  correlated with the error are  automatically highlighted  also um you must be aware that actually  a model performance predictor  is a predictor of performances so you  can also use it as a model  on new data that is not uh labeled  and you could get hints about um your  primary model performance on this new  data  like for instance in this example you  could say a look those observations your  model will probably fail here  and this is useful information in  particular if we put all together so we  have a train model for a start  and we fit a decision tree on the errors  in the test set to build our performance  predictor  then we can have a look at the segment  at the failures notes and  we can have a look at the features that  are more correlated with the error in  those failure nodes  at this point we have two options either  we  gather enough information to go back to  model design or maybe to improve our  data collection process or we can still  use  our model of new data to predict  possible  failure on some data and then we can  select this data and send for instance  for manual inspection instead  so here i put some references for the  model performance predictor for the  couriers readers  but you can also ask any question you  have in mind now  and thanks for watching

